"At the bottom of his doubt Descartes finds one thing the demon cannot take away. Let the demon deceive him as much as it can: to be deceived he must exist. This proposition I am, I exist, is necessarily true each time it is expressed by me, or conceived in my mind. The point is usually called the cogito, from the Latin for I think. Notice how little it gives him. It tells him that he exists for as long as he is thinking, and that he is at least a thing that thinks. It does not yet tell him that he has a body, that he has a past, or that anything exists outside his mind. Descartes spent the remaining four Meditations trying to win the world back, by way of a proof that God exists and is no deceiver. Few readers have been persuaded by that route. The doubt has proved more durable than the cure.": "6503b6d01375",
"Buddhist texts reach for the dream constantly. The Diamond Sūtra ends by advising that all conditioned things be viewed as a dream, an illusion, a bubble, a shadow (一切有為法，如夢幻泡影). The comparison is put to philosophical work in Vasubandhus Twenty Verses, written in India in the fourth or fifth century. Vasubandhu holds that what we experience is consciousness only, and he faces an obvious objection: if there were no external objects, why would experiences be tied to particular places and times? He answers with the dream. In a dream too we see a village or a garden at one place and not everywhere, at one time and not always, though no external village is there (處時定如夢). The orderliness of experience therefore does not prove that it has an outside cause.": "e6064eeb3f9a",
"Even so, something seems to hold out against the dream. Whether he is awake or asleep, two and three make five, and a square has but four sides. For these he needs a third and more radical supposition: that some malignant demon, who is at once exceedingly potent and deceitful, has employed all his artifice to deceive me, so that he goes wrong even when he adds. Nobody believes in the demon, and Descartes did not either. Its job is to mark the outer limit of what can coherently be doubted.": "37c25341e5c5",
"Everyone has had the experience of waking and finding that what seemed real a moment ago was not. While it lasted, the dream did not feel like a dream. The falling felt like falling, and the examination for which one had not prepared was as alarming as a real one. This raises a question that sounds childish and turns out to be very hard. If a dream can be that convincing from the inside, what tells you that you are awake now? Whatever test you propose, pinching yourself, checking a clock, asking a friend, you could dream that you performed it and dream that you passed.": "3537a43e0155",
"For all I know, I might be dreaming.": "ca36bc132d03",
"He noticed how many false opinions he had taken for granted in his youth.": "fe9f34145b7a",
"He resolves to demolish everything and begin again from the foundations.": "447d4f454b4b",
"He tried to win the world back by way of a proof that God exists.": "27aac50c3f77",
"He will set aside any belief for which he can find a ground of doubt.": "33b38f86d8a6",
"I perceive so clearly that there exist no certain marks by which the state of waking can ever be distinguished from sleep, that I feel greatly astonished; and in amazement I almost persuade myself that I am now dreaming.": "d2e920b13ce3",
"It is tempting to say that none of this could make any practical difference, and in one sense that is true. You will not tell dreaming and waking apart any better after reading Descartes. What changes is your picture of what knowing is. Before the dream argument it is natural to think of knowledge as a direct grasp of how things are. After it, one has to choose. Either knowledge demands a certainty that almost nothing can meet, or it is something more modest: true belief that is well supported and could, in principle, have been wrong. Most philosophers today take the second view. So do the sciences, which hold even their best theories open to revision. The dream argument is where that modesty was first learned.": "f39a41c3b4ae",
"Nearly two thousand years earlier, the Chinese philosopher Zhuangzi had told the most famous dream in literature. He dreamed he was a butterfly, fluttering about, content, with no idea that he was Zhuang Zhou. Suddenly he woke, and there he was, solidly Zhuang Zhou. He did not know whether he was Zhou who had dreamed he was a butterfly, or a butterfly dreaming he was Zhou.": "203cef04b43e",
"One belief survives when everything else has been called into question.": "5eba3a7d7c41",
"René Descartes made this question the starting point of modern philosophy. In the Meditations of 1641 he describes himself as a man who has noticed how many false opinions he took for granted in his youth, and who resolves, once in his life, to demolish everything and begin again from the foundations. His instrument is doubt, used as a test. He will set aside any belief for which he can find the slightest ground of doubt, in order to see whether anything is left. It is important to understand that he does not think his beliefs are false. He treats them the way one treats a basket of apples when some may be rotten: tip them all out, and put back only those that pass inspection.": "8cb82af57469",
"So far this is Descartes second wave. The difference lies in what follows. Descartes treats the dream as a threat and brings in God to remove it. Vasubandhu accepts the comparison and draws a practical conclusion: ordinary waking life is itself a kind of sleep, from which one can wake. That is the literal meaning of buddha, one who has awakened, and it is very close to Zhuangzis great awakening. On the cogito the traditions part more sharply. A Buddhist philosopher grants that thinking is going on and denies the step to a thinker. What Descartes found at the bottom of his doubt and took for a substance, the Buddhist analysis takes apart into a stream of momentary events with no owner. Lichtenbergs objection is, in effect, the doctrine of no-self (Dharma and the West, D2 and D11).": "8c697f3b805e",
"So that it must, in fine, be maintained, all things being maturely and carefully considered, that this proposition (pronunciatum) I am, I exist, is necessarily true each time it is expressed by me, or conceived in my mind.": "bc4baf13bd07",
"Something seems to hold out against the dream.": "bf8fad9fabbb",
"The doubt comes in three waves, each stronger than the last. First, the senses sometimes deceive: a square tower looks round from far away. But this touches only what is distant or small. It gives no reason to doubt, as he says, that I am in this place, seated by the fire, clothed in a winter dressing gown. The second wave reaches that as well. He has often dreamed of sitting by the fire while he lay undressed in bed, and on reflection he finds that there exist no certain marks by which the state of waking can ever be distinguished from sleep.": "b6f8fb1560dc",
"The doubt has proved more durable than the cure.": "ed7df7c19ab7",
"The position that we cannot know anything about the external world is called skepticism, and three kinds of reply are worth knowing. The first is G. E. Moores. In a lecture of 1939 he held up his hands and said, in effect: here is one hand, and here is another; so there are external things. He granted that he could not prove he was not dreaming. His point was that he was far more certain of his hands than he could ever be of the premises of any philosophical argument to the contrary, so it was the argument that had to go.": "cf5abd065493",
"The premise is Descartes and the mood is entirely different. Descartes is alarmed and wants certainty back. Zhuangzi is unworried. He adds that between Zhou and the butterfly there must be some distinction, and calls this the transformation of things. Elsewhere in the same chapter he suggests that only after a great awakening will we know that all this was a great dream, while fools believe that they are already awake. For him the dream is a way of loosening our confidence in fixed distinctions: self and other, waking and sleeping, perhaps life and death.": "381e23e7e0ba",
"The sciences hold even their best theories open to revision.": "784f08bb558d",
"The second reply denies that knowledge requires certainty. On this view, called fallibilism, I know that I am awake if my belief is true and is produced in a reliable way, even though I cannot rule out every imaginable alternative. For all I know in the skeptics sense, I might be dreaming; in the ordinary sense, I know perfectly well that I am not. The third reply questions whether the skeptics story makes sense. Hilary Putnam argued in 1981 that a brain which had always been kept in a vat and fed its experiences by a computer could not even think the thought I am a brain in a vat, because its words would never have been connected to real brains and vats. None of these replies is accepted by everyone, which is why the dream is still discussed in every introductory course.": "3f3217e59293",
"You will not tell dreaming and waking apart any better after reading Descartes.": "b6802661a41f",
